one month after he became only the third  u.s. president to be impeached formal  proceedings against Donald Trump have  begun in the US Senate within the past  hour the charges against him have been  read out in the Senate Trump is charged  with abuse of power for threatening to  withhold military aid to Ukraine in  exchange for a political favor he's also  charged with obstruction of Congress the  house managers of the US House of  Representatives outlined the charges  during an official reading of the  articles of impeachment the trial itself  is expected to start next week Donald J  Trump has abused the powers of the  presidency in that using the powers of  his high office President Trump  solicited the interference of a foreign  government Ukraine in the 2020 United  States presidential election he did so  through a scheme or course of conduct  that included soliciting the government  of Ukraine to publicly announce  investigations that would benefit his  reelection harm the election prospects  of a political opponent and influence  the 2020 United States presidential  election to his advantage let's go live  now to Capitol Hill Al Jazeera's Heidi  Joe Castro is there for us so Heidi  we're off talk us through what we saw  over the last hour or so that formal  walk of the house managers from the  house through to the Senate  that's right and it wasn't just the the  motion that they were going through  really that walk represents a key  threshold in this impeachment process  transferring the power of impeaching  President Trump from the US House of  Representatives which of course is the  Democratic controlled chamber and  transferring that power now to the  Senate a very different environment this  is where we have the Republican majority  which is of course President Trump's own  party they will conduct the trial of  President Trump and officially this  trial will kick off in just about an  hour's time that is when Chief judges  Justice John Roberts of the US Supreme  Court will be escorted into the Senate  chambers he will be administered and  oath to become the presiding officer of  this trial and then he will immediately  in turn swear in the 100 senators to  become the impartial jurist to decide  whether or not President Trump remains  in office  now that word impartial has been much  dissected in the sense because the very  leader of this body Mitch McConnell the  top Republican in the Senate he started  off by saying that he had no intention  of being impartial that he wanted he and  his members to defend the president and  his claim of innocence well regardless  they will be taking this oath and then  starting on next Tuesday that is when we  expect opening statements from both  sides  Democrats documenting what they say is  Trump's guilty actions of withholding US  security aid to Ukraine in order to  pressure its government to announce  investigations into Trump's political  opponents here in the US the president  has said he will send his attorneys to  defend him in those opening arguments  after that we don't know if they could  call for more witnesses that's something  that Democrats certainly want but they  won't have that happen without at least  some Republican support how do you many  thanks indeed let's separate a White  House correspondent Kimberly halket what  reaction if any has there been to all of  this from the White House Kimberly yeah  a couple of important tweets from the  White House press secretary Stephanie  Grisham who has said  in just the last half-hour obstruction  of Congress of course one of the  articles against the president she says  the speaker holding the articles for  four weeks before sending them to the US  Senate and the other article against the  president she responds to abuse of power  the house refusing to give the president  due process anyone noticed the Democrats  always accused POTUS that's President of  the United States of actions they  themselves are guilty of doing question  mark exclamation point it's safe to say  that the White House is doing what it  can to discredit the proceedings that  will kick off on Tuesday we expect in  the US Senate  Stephanie Grisham the White House press  secretary also trying to get out there  in the headlines what should have been a  shining moment for the US president  that's the passage of a major trade deal  that he renegotiated with Mexico and  Canada to replace NAFTA that allowed for  many manufacturing jobs to evaporate in  this country it was a major campaign  promise it also got passed in the US  Senate a but no one's talking about it  except the press secretary who writes  after yesterday's historic trade deal  with China and the u.s. MCA passed today  the Dems are now reading their sham  articles of impeachment which they  manufactured she says in order to stop  real Donald Trump from his conceit  continued success on behalf of the  United States  he's working there whining so again some  of sort of that nasty language being  used to try and discredit the process  that is going on in the US Senate  against US president Donald Trump on  White House correspondent Kimberly  halket live for us there in Washington  Alan Barron is a former special  impeachment counsel to the House of  Representatives he was involved in the  impeachment and removal of for federal  judges Allen joins us now live from  Washington Alan are those articles of  impeachment manufactured as the press  secretary claims no they are all based  on the evidence that was presented in  the house indeed with regard to the both  articles there's really no dispute as to  the fact I don't think anybody has an  illusions that as to whether or not the  facts that are alleged in the articles  whether in fact those are true what it's  really going to boil down  is to raw political power in the Senate  who has the votes but there's no  ambiguity as to what the facts are in  both instances so what sort of trial  will this be will Republican senators be  impartial as they were required in fact  they will swear an oath to be the answer  is no they may may they have the fig  leaf so to speak of the oath but even  though they may not have committed  themselves ahead of time as to how  they're going to vote we can expect that  there were only few if any defections  from the Republican ranks on the merits  it is perhaps unfortunate that the oath  that they take really puts them in this  difficult position but reality tells you  that their positions are pretty well  fixed before they hear one word of  evidence and what do you think of the  the articles of impeachment themselves  when they were read out by by Adam  Schiff well they're they're very  powerful I mean it was very solemn the  way mr. Schiff presented them and anyone  listening to that who had not completely  prejudged that this was all you know a  sham and and and should not be taking  place you had to be impressed with the  effects that were being laid out there  that this really goes to the heart of  the presidency at the power of the  presidency to engage a foreign  government with regard to the first  article and to use that relationship for  his own political purposes and the  second article which has taken sort of a  backseat a little bit but in fact it  subverts the entire constitutional  process of impeachment  it basically says you can't Stonewall  the house when it's trying to carry out  its constitutional duty and that is the  essence of what the second article  alleges and what about today's  revelation that an associate of the  president's own lawyer has been saying  that that the president was only  was with all too aware of what was going  on is that going to make any difference  well if the Republicans do not allow new  testimony new evidence to come in I  think they will do that at their peril  they may not pay for it here in this  proceeding in this impeachment trial but  I believe they will pay for it at the  ballot box in